MreS9DDFL__uzwxkhjoC9Jr6UZNNnw3f-hefieo3lUUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;wzhOYK6J7LuglZAPYMFc04VAYAVn1RdM7NtqIJ7yA50ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke32000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;WvCjpA3m4GGxke8xbQSLyHki2Ya5zNrw_CesNPgWz6Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd4621ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;r8Rw3Y3QohICsAQYTnArAQUyxsqNtNMPjdgaeUL4mTMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;PCjZWebSAWphTz_IOj-VNL9UQLUlyR3EJYQSsV-WAN0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;BCkOlTPBhhDOSefDR_wt3YQ9hXeDzEU-qAXxWAuy6GYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;36BQo8gZaSs9SMJs2zgUqLyFQOua2q0MBHHlfvom0_Yƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky—A new collection edited by Edward Fullbrook and Jamie Morgan, [23 Responses to Economics' Biggest-ever Question](https://rwer.wordpress.com/2026/07/14/23-responses-to-economics-biggest-ever-question/), situates the limits to growth â€” as an intellectual problem, a policy problem, and a disciplinary crisis â€” on the table in terms the economics profession cannot easily dismiss. Subtitled "Economics and the Biophysical Limits to Economic Growth," the volume's framing rests on a claim made by natural science: the global economy caused the climate crisis, and the crisis, now in an accelerating phase, threatens the economy's own existence. For a discipline that has "loosely guided the global economy's national economies" for over a century, that is not a refinement. It is a structural challenge.

## The Policy Architecture Already Built on a Bet

The difficulty is that major institutional decisions have not waited for this reckoning. Green growth â€” the premise that economic expansion can be decoupled from resource use and made consistent with ecological stability â€” is not a fringe hypothesis. [Green growth](https://unevenearth.org/2022/10/green-growth/) by Winne van Woerden for Uneven Earth documents it as the governing assumption embedded in the European Green Deal, the Paris Agreement, IPCC reports, and the US Inflation Reduction Act, promoted by leading multilateral organizations and treated as common sense across national and international policymaking.

That consensus rests on a specific mechanism: decoupling, the idea that economic output can continue to grow while its ecological costs fall. In practice, this has meant reliance on net zero frameworks â€” scenarios in which continued emissions are balanced by future removal of carbon from the atmosphere, predominantly through technological approaches. The promise is that growth does not need to stop; it only needs to clean up after itself, eventually. Van Woerden argues that the empirical basis for that promise is far weaker than the confidence currently invested in it.

The institutional bet embedded in those frameworks is that the answer to the collection's central question is yes â€” growth can continue, cleanly enough, within biophysical limits. If the 23 responses suggest otherwise, the implications don't settle in academic journals. They land in every policy instrument that has already staked its architecture on decoupling working at the required scale.

## The Ecomodernist Premise and Its Discontents

Green growth is not simply an optimistic reading of the data. It rests on a specific intellectual foundation: an ecomodernist belief in technological progress holding that there are no limits to growth because there are no limits to human ingenuity. From this premise, the limits-to-growth tradition represents not a scientific constraint to be accommodated but a failure of imagination to be overcome.

Van Woerden's analysis for Uneven Earth argues that this foundation is dangerously misguided, and that the decoupling concept on which green growth's credibility depends does not hold up to scrutiny. Part of the argument turns on a distinction between relative and absolute decoupling. Relative decoupling â€” where emissions rise more slowly than the economy â€” has been observed in some wealthy nations. But what the green growth framework requires is absolute decoupling: total emissions falling even as the economy expands. The empirical record on this, Van Woerden argues, does not support the confidence placed in it.

A second line of critique concerns what Van Woerden calls the Jevons paradox. Efficiency improvements in how resources are used tend, in growth-oriented economies, to lower costs and stimulate more consumption rather than reduce total resource use. The anticipated gains from greener technologies are absorbed by expanded production and demand, not banked as ecological savings. This means that scaling up clean technology, without addressing the growth dynamic itself, transforms what is intended as an energy transition into an energy expansion in practice.

A third concern is what Van Woerden describes as "carbon tunnel" thinking â€” the tendency of green growth frameworks to focus on reducing atmospheric carbon through technological innovation while treating that as a solution to the broader ecological problem. Earth scientists identify multiple interacting natural systems, not carbon alone, as the relevant boundaries. Policies that assume carbon removal can substitute for addressing the full range of ecological pressures are, on this account, solving a narrower problem than the one that actually exists.

## The Post-Growth Agenda and What the Volume's Arrival Changes

The Fullbrook and Morgan collection arrives not into an open disciplinary debate but into one in which major institutions have already committed their architecture to the bet that growth and ecological stability can be reconciled. What is at stake is not a hypothesis still under review but the operative logic of emissions targets, net zero timelines, and technology scaling plans already in effect.

Van Woerden, writing for Uneven Earth, notes that an increasing number of scientists are calling for a post-growth climate mitigation agenda â€” one grounded in the full range of interacting natural systems rather than the carbon-specific framing of current frameworks. What the economics discipline, whose century of guidance the Fullbrook and Morgan collection examines, makes of that call is the unresolved question the volume puts on the table; the policy frameworks built on that guidance are already operative, and 23 formal responses now mark the point at which the disciplinary challenge to their foundations can no longer be treated as peripheral.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ILrqVFjJghSSGnIUuZeJ5w_nXUlK25iTxPXqcS8KUu0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$21ca34f3-2267-42a3-b17a-b5b2f3e3eba3ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;vYbnvnPGSsnoxS3PBhL0oG0tpimEHMZwkm9X2cPLRnQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhend_turnƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;yiZmZFtlRAIV75m54_uyXtnp79QiMGW9cOTDfL7hdscƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgsuccessƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;6IjjrXe--puNW2DN9xIr3EOsvB5RyAknps9b-bqr7rMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkicompletedƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;-xoI4HqXAWIu9RDKdbbMPEKCk-KBistHrDTN1RNzNhUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkb20ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Fq4Opl9c_yx6YhGF_6sG4gi7CNcc7g-VdXGHJ8pstTcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarki0.1731393ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8hf2Tukhbwce0YsguFcB789ZJm5b47iS5U3a-VSacWsƒdpairƒe